Monthly Mutt Counter (MMC)
A personal, online, month-by-month “mutth” counter with one-way increments, automatic calendar-month rollover, and a permanent read-only history view.
Project status: Version 1.0 is deployed and usable (November 2026). Password login, online incrementing, refresh persistence, and history-page navigation were tested. The first real calendar-month rollover has not yet occurred or been end-to-end verified.

1. Project idea and purpose
Monthly Mutt Counter is a small, private-in-use personal tracker for counting occurrences of “mutth” (the project's chosen term). The owner opens the website, signs in with an email and password, and presses +1 Increase each time an occurrence should be recorded. The number shown is the count for the current calendar month, not the lifetime total.

The design intentionally does not offer a minus-one or reset button: ordinary website users should not be able to reduce their count or rewrite previous months. When a new month begins in the Asia/Dhaka timezone, the main counter shows 0 until that month's first increment. Previous months retain their own database totals and appear on the History page after completion.

This is a simple personal logging tool, not a medical tool, diagnosis, or recommendation about how often someone should engage in any behavior.
Core requirements
- One clear, large current-month number.
- A single +1 Increase control; no decrease or manual reset control in the published site.
- Normal email-and-password sign-in for everyday use.
- All counts saved online in Supabase PostgreSQL, not in browser localStorage.
- Rollover on actual calendar-month boundaries, including 28/29/30/31-day months and year changes.
- History shows completed months only, with totals displayed read-only.
- Store a small amount of data over many years: approximately 12 rows per year per user with recorded activity, or 240 monthly rows over 20 years if each month has a row.
- Same dark navy design and looping water-ring, droplet, and bubble animations across pages.
- Develop and test locally in VS Code before pushing updates to GitHub.
